## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates practical concurrency control skills involving multiple producers, bounded buffers, and latency guarantees with a simple priority policy. The solution tests real-world challenges like starvation prevention and graceful shutdown coordination.

## Key Concepts

- Dual-queue architecture for priority separation
- Mutual exclusion and condition synchronization
- Backpressure mechanisms for flow control
- Aging algorithms to prevent starvation
- Graceful shutdown patterns with resource cleanup

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo sketch of data structures and worker loop
class Task { 
  constructor(priority) { 
    this.priority = priority; 
    this.timestamp = Date.now();
  } 
}

class PriorityQueue {
  constructor(capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.highQueue = [];
    this.normalQueue = [];
    this.mutex = new Mutex();
    this.notEmpty = new ConditionVariable();
    this.notFull = new ConditionVariable();
    this.shutdown = false;
    this.agingThreshold = 5000; // 5 seconds
  }

  async enqueue(task) {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    while (this.size() >= this.capacity && !this.shutdown) {
      await this.notFull.wait();
    }

    if (task.priority === 'high') {
      this.highQueue.push(task);
    } else {
      this.normalQueue.push(task);
    }

    this.notEmpty.signal();
    this.mutex.unlock();
  }

  async dequeue() {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    while (this.size() === 0 && !this.shutdown) {
      await this.notEmpty.wait();
    }

    if (this.shutdown && this.size() === 0) {
      this.mutex.unlock();
      return null;
    }

    this.checkAging();

    let task;
    if (this.highQueue.length > 0) {
      task = this.highQueue.shift();
    } else if (this.normalQueue.length > 0) {
      task = this.normalQueue.shift();
    }

    this.notFull.signal();
    this.mutex.unlock();
    return task;
  }

  checkAging() {
    const now = Date.now();
    this.normalQueue = this.normalQueue.filter(task => {
      if (now - task.timestamp > this.agingThreshold) {
        this.highQueue.push(task);
        return false;
      }
      return true;
    });
  }

  size() {
    return this.highQueue.length + this.normalQueue.length;
  }

  async shutdown() {
    await this.mutex.lock();
    this.shutdown = true;
    this.notEmpty.broadcast();
    this.notFull.broadcast();
    this.mutex.unlock();
  }
}
```

## Test Plan

### Latency Testing
- Measure end-to-end processing time for high vs. normal priority tasks
- Verify high-priority tasks consistently achieve lower latency
- Test with varying load levels to validate priority guarantees

### Starvation Testing
- Generate continuous high-priority load and monitor normal task processing
- Verify aging mechanism promotes aged normal tasks
- Measure maximum wait time for normal tasks under sustained high load

### Graceful Shutdown Testing
- Initiate shutdown while tasks are processing
- Verify all in-flight tasks complete before worker termination
- Test with producers blocked on backpressure
- Confirm no resource leaks or deadlocks

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design a bounded, low-latency queue with per-partition ordering, backpressure, and graceful shutdown. It also probes fault-tolerance concepts like spill-to-disk for handling overflow scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- Bounded ring buffer with atomic indices for lock-free operations
- Per-partition ordering guarantees using sequence numbers
- Lock-free or low-lock synchronization with proper memory ordering
- Backpressure strategies to prevent resource exhaustion
- Graceful shutdown coordination with worker notification
- Spill-to-disk path for overflow handling

## Code Example
```go
// Simplified data structures (illustrative)
type Entry struct {
  PartitionID int
  Sequence   uint64
  Data       []byte
}

type RingBuffer struct {
  Buffer    []Entry
  Capacity   int64
  Head       int64  // Consumer position
  Tail       int64  // Producer position
  Mask       int64  // Capacity-1 for modulo operations
  Full       bool
  Shutdown    int32  // Atomic shutdown flag
}

func (rb *RingBuffer) Enqueue(entry Entry) error {
  for {
    head := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Head)
    tail := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Head)

    if (tail-head) >= rb.Capacity {
      // Buffer full, apply backpressure or spill to disk
      return rb.spillToDisk(entry)
    }

    // Attempt to advance tail using CAS
    newTail := (tail + 1) & rb.Mask
    if atomic.CompareAndSwapInt64(&rb.Tail, tail, newTail) {
      rb.Buffer[tail] = entry
      return nil
    }
  }
}

func (rb *RingBuffer) Dequeue() (Entry, bool) {
  for {
    if atomic.LoadInt32(&rb.Shutdown) == 1 {
      return Entry{}, false
    }

    head := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Head)
    tail := atomic.LoadInt64(&rb.Tail)

    if head == tail {
      return Entry{}, false  // Empty
    }

    // Attempt to advance head using CAS
    newHead := (head + 1) & rb.Mask
    if atomic.CompareAndSwapInt64(&rb.Head, head, newHead) {
      return rb.Buffer[head], true
    }
  }
}
```

